="nofollow">Twitter for Android</a>', 'quoted_status_result': {'result': {'__typename': 'Tweet', 'rest_id': '1876659991702352228', 'core': {'user_results': {'result': {'__typename': 'User', 'id': 'VXNlcjo5NDIzNTgxMzY=', 'rest_id': '942358136', 'affiliates_highlighted_label': {}, 'has_graduated_access': True, 'is_blue_verified': True, 'profile_image_shape': 'Circle', 'legacy': {'can_dm': True, 'can_media_tag': True, 'created_at': 'Sun Nov 11 22:21:47 +0000 2012', 'default_profile': True, 'default_profile_image': False, 'description': 'Founder: Social Media Freedom Foundation 501c3. Section 230 expert consultant. Published author and media personality. Fyk vs. Facebook / Fyk vs. United States', 'entities': {'description': {'urls': []}, 'url': {'urls': [{'display_url': 'socialmediafreedom.org', 'expanded_url': 'https://www.socialmediafreedom.org', 'url': 'https://t.co/qmUBBj7DLb', 'indices': [0, 23]}]}}, 'fast_followers_count': 0, 'favourites_count': 4741, 'followers_count': 38763, 'friends_count': 940, 'has_custom_timelines': True, 'is_translator': False, 'listed_count': 89, 'location': 'USA', 'media_count': 1494, 'name': 'Jason Fyk', 'normal_followers_count': 38763, 'pinned_tweet_ids_str': ['1912258934913876215'], 'possibly_sensitive': False, 'profile_banner_url': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_banners/942358136/1609246862', 'profile_image_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1348698835065987072/m5qGv_Df_normal.jpg', 'profile_interstitial_type': '', 'screen_name': 'JasonFyk', 'statuses_count': 13315, 'translator_type': 'none', 'url': 'https://t.co/qmUBBj7DLb', 'verified': False, 'want_retweets': False, 'withheld_in_countries': []}}}}, 'unmention_data': {}, 'edit_control': {'edit_tweet_ids': ['1876659991702352228'], 'editable_until_msecs': '1736269183000', 'is_edit_eligible': True, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'is_translatable': False, 'views': {'count': '44305', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="http://twitter.com/download/android" rel="nofollow">Twitter for Android</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4NzY2NTk5OTEzMzMxODc1ODU=', 'text': 'Big Tech’s Preemptive Business Model Changes and Accountability\n\nMark Zuckerberg\'s recent announcement of major changes to Meta’s operations has sparked widespread speculation about the true motivation. While framed as a response to First Amendment principles or platform evolution, the reality may be far deeper. For over two decades, California courts have misapplied Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act, granting platforms unprecedented immunity Congress never intended. My fight to realign Section 230(c)(1) with its original text, the "Good Samaritan" intent, and constitutional principles has exposed a judicial system that systematically shields Big Tech. Now, after years of advocacy, these business model shifts appear to be a direct response to the looming inevitability of accountability.\n\nThe Misapplication of Section 230\n\nCalifornia courts made a pivotal grammatical error in interpreting Section 230, turning it into a blanket shield of absolute immunity. Platforms have been allowed to act as publishers while evading responsibility for content decisions—actions that should never have been protected under 230(c)(1). Over the last seven years, I’ve challenged this misinterpretation, showing how it undermines due process and the Constitution. Despite persistent resistance, this fight has laid bare the systemic bias enabling Big Tech’s unchecked power.\n\nJudicial Bias and Systemic Failures\n\nAs detailed in my recent en banc petition, the courts have not just misapplied the law but have done so in ethically questionable ways:\n\nConflicts of Interest: A federal judge in my case held millions in tech stocks. He ruled in favor of Facebook, preserving his investments, before recusing himself—five years too late.\n\nInconsistent Rulings: The Ninth Circuit continues to ignore its own precedent and conflicting rulings from other circuits. Cases like Henderson v. Public Data and Anderson v. TikTok show how other courts correctly apply Section 230, starkly contrasting with California’s judiciary.\n\nThese systemic issues have deprived me of justice while allowing Big Tech to weaponize Section 230 against competition and free speech. This is no longer just a fight against Facebook; it’s a battle against a judiciary that has entrenched itself as Big Tech’s protector.\n\nTurning the tide\n\nThe sudden shifts in Big Tech’s business models seem directly tied to growing public and legal pressures. It’s increasingly clear these companies recognize the inevitability of accountability. By reorienting now, they aim to soften the blow when the absurd immunity shield they’ve exploited for decades finally falls. This aligns with the arguments raised in my en banc petition.\n\nThe Larger Implications\n\nThe misuse of Section 230 has profound consequences:\n\n1. Censorship and Free Speech: Platforms use immunity to suppress speech under the guise of moderation, as seen in cases like Missouri v. Biden.\n\n2. Economic Harm: Small businesses and creators like myself—and millions of users—have suffered from unchecked anti-competitive practices.\n\n3. Judicial Complicity: Courts’ refusal to correct these misinterpretations has turned the internet into a "lawless no-man’s-land."\n\nWhy This Matters\n\nIf Section 230 is applied as written, platforms will face accountability for their moderation decisions, forcing fundamental changes to how they operate. Accountability is critical to restoring fairness, protecting free speech, and dismantling the censorship industrial complex. Fixing Section 230 would not only resolve these issues but set a precedent for competition and constitutional adherence.\n\nThis fight has been grueling, but it’s yielding results. Big Tech’s preemptive changes signal that reform is inevitable. As detailed in my pinned post, the courts now face a defining choice: perpetuate injustice or align Section 230 with the law, the Constitution, and public interest. Justice is on trial as much as Big Tech.\n\nhttps://t.co/GcjX5MHzDr', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [{'display_url': 'x.com/JasonFyk/statu…', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/JasonFyk/status/1871558978079187206?s=19', 'url': 'https://t.co/GcjX5MHzDr', 'indices': [3941, 3964]}], 'user_mentions': []}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 45, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Tue Jan 07 15:59:43 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1876659991702352228', 'display_text_range': [0, 271], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}, 'favorite_count': 155, 'favorited': False, 'full_text': "Big Tech’s Preemptive Business Model Changes and Accountability\n\nMark Zuckerberg's recent announcement of major changes to Meta’s operations has sparked widespread speculation about the true motivation. While framed as a response to First Amendment principles or platform", 'is_quote_status': False, 'lang': 'en', 'quote_count': 13, 'reply_count': 18, 'retweet_count': 72, 'retweeted': False, 'user_id_str': '942358136', 'id_str': '1876659991702352228'}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Tue Jan 07 16:14:16 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1876615055384387812', 'display_text_range': [14, 99], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [{'display_url': 'x.com/JasonFyk/statu…', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/JasonFyk/status/1876659991702352228?s=19', 'url': 'https://t.co/yaykXUUY3y', 'indices': [76, 99]}], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}]}, 'favorite_count': 2, 'favorited': False, 'full_text': "@TheChiefNerd If you want to know why they're changing, here is the reason. https://t.co/yaykXUUY3y", 'in_reply_to_screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'in_reply_